+# JWT Claims Reference
+
+Claims passed in the JWT used to sign a Sigma embed URL. Some belong in the header, some in the payload. Source: [Sigma JSON web token claims reference](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/docs/json-web-token-claims-reference).
+
+## Required Claims
+
+| Claim | Where | Type | Description |
+|-------|-------|------|-------------|
+| `sub` | payload | string | Email address of the embed user. Must be RFC-1035 compliant (no whitespace, no underscores). |
+| `jti` | payload | string | Unique ID associated with the session. Generate a fresh UUID per session. |
+| `iat` | payload | number | Issued-at time as Unix seconds. |
+| `exp` | payload | number | Expiration as Unix seconds. `exp - iat` must be ≤ 30 days (2592000 seconds). Default 1 hour is typical. |
+| `kid` | header  | string | The embed client ID. Must be in the header, not the payload. |
+
+## Optional Claims
+
+### Identity / metadata
+
+| Claim | Where | Type | Notes |
+|-------|-------|------|-------|
+| `alg` | header | string | Signing algorithm — must be `HS256`. Most JWT libraries set this implicitly. |
+| `iss` | payload | string | Issuer. Set equal to the embed client ID. |
+| `first_name`, `last_name` | payload | string | Display name for auto-created embed users. **Do not set for internal users.** |
+
+### Permission control (auto-created embed users only)
+
+| Claim | Where | Type | Notes |
+|-------|-------|------|-------|
+| `account_type` | payload | string | Account type for the embed user. If omitted, Sigma defaults to the highest available account type. **Do not set for internal users.** |
+| `teams` | payload | string array | Teams the embed user joins. Format: `["team1", "team2"]`. Team names with spaces/special characters must **not** be URL-encoded. **Do not set for internal users.** |
+| `user_attributes` | payload | `Record<string,string>` | User attribute name/value pairs. Drives RLS, CLS, and dynamic warehouse role assignment. Format: `{"attribute1":"value1"}`. **Do not set for internal users.** |
+
+### Data access
+
+| Claim | Where | Type | Notes |
+|-------|-------|------|-------|
+| `oauth_token` | payload | string | OAuth token for organization-level OAuth connections. Must be encrypted with the embed secret — see the [Sigma Node.js Embed SDK](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sigmacomputing/node-embed-sdk). Requires `ver: "1.1"`. |
+| `connection_oauth_tokens` | payload | `Record<string,string>` | Per-connection OAuth tokens. Keys are connection IDs, values are encrypted tokens. Requires `ver: "1.1"`. |
+| `eval_connection_id` | payload | string | Connection ID to use instead of the workbook's default. Useful for per-tenant connection switching. **Not** compatible with OAuth connections or write-back features (input tables, materialization, Python). |
+
+### Multi-org / versioning
+
+| Claim | Where | Type | Notes |
+|-------|-------|------|-------|
+| `tenant` | payload | string | UUID of the tenant organization (Sigma Tenants Beta). Requires `ver: "1.1"`. |
+| `ver` | payload | string | JWT version — `"1.0"` (default) or `"1.1"`. Required `"1.1"` if using `oauth_token`, `connection_oauth_tokens`, or `tenant`. |
+| `aud` | payload | string | Audience. Required when `ver: "1.1"` — must be `"sigmacomputing"`. Ignored otherwise. |
+
+## Internal vs Embed Users
+
+Internal users (people with a Sigma login) bring their own account type, teams, and user attributes from Sigma — those values are managed inside Sigma, not via JWT.
+
+For internal users, **only** set: `sub`, `jti`, `iat`, `exp`, `kid`, optionally `iss`, optionally `eval_connection_id`. Omit `account_type`, `teams`, `first_name`, `last_name`, and `user_attributes`.
+
+For auto-created embed users (no Sigma login), set the identity, permission, and data-access claims as needed.
+
+## Critical Constraint
+
+> JWT claims are specific to a **user**, not a **session**. Use consistent claim values for the same embed user across different sessions. Do not use claims (especially `teams`, `account_type`, `user_attributes`) to gate what the same user sees in different sessions — that should be managed at the user/team level in Sigma.
+
+If a single application user genuinely needs different views in different sessions, treat them as separate Sigma users (different `sub` emails).
+
+## Example — Auto-created embed user with RLS + warehouse role switching
+
+```js
+{
+  sub: 'user@customer-a.example',
+  iss: process.env.SIGMA_CLIENT_ID,
+  jti: uuid(),
+  iat: now,
+  exp: now + 3600,
+  account_type: 'Viewer',
+  teams: ['Customer A'],
+  user_attributes: {
+    Region: 'West',
+    SnowflakeRole: 'CUSTOMER_A',
+  },
+}
+```
+
+## Example — Internal user (has a Sigma account)
+
+```js
+{
+  sub: 'employee@company.example',
+  iss: process.env.SIGMA_CLIENT_ID,
+  jti: uuid(),
+  iat: now,
+  exp: now + 3600,
+  // No account_type, teams, or user_attributes — managed in Sigma.
+}
+```
