+# Managing Users in a Secure Embed
+
+Two models for who can access a Sigma embed. Source: [Sigma: Manage access to a secure embed](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/docs/manage-access-to-a-secure-embed).
+
+## Automatic vs Manual Provisioning
+
+|  | Automatic embed-user creation (default) | Manual user management |
+|--|----------------------------------------|------------------------|
+| Who manages identity | Host application is the IdP | Sigma or an external IdP |
+| User lifecycle | Sigma auto-creates an account from the JWT's `sub` (email) on first access | User must already exist in Sigma |
+| Permission control | JWT claims (`teams`, `account_type`, `user_attributes`) | Set on the user/team in Sigma |
+| When to choose | Most SaaS apps. Simpler, less duplication of user state. | When you already provision Sigma accounts via SSO/SCIM and want a single source of truth. |
+
+To **disable** automatic creation: **Administration → Embeds → Settings → Allow automatic embed user creation** (admin only).
+
+> If automatic creation is off and an internal user attempts to access the embed before logging into Sigma at least once, they'll see *"You don't have permission to access this page. Automatic updating of users is disabled."* Have them log in to Sigma first.
+
+## Required Setup Steps
+
+1. **Generate embed credentials** — `Administration → Developer access → Create new` with **Embedding** scope.
+2. **Create a team** for the embed audience.
+   - UI: [Manage teams](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/docs/manage-teams).
+   - API: [Create a team](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/reference/createteam).
+3. **Decide on access model** (above).
+4. **Grant the team access** to the workbook (or, ideally, to a workspace containing the workbook).
+   - UI: [Share a folder](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/docs/share-a-folder).
+   - API: [Create or update a grant](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/reference/creategrant).
+5. **Grant the team access to the data sources** the workbook depends on.
+   - UI: [Manage data permissions](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/docs/manage-data-permissions).
+   - API: [Create or update a grant](https://help.sigmacomputing.com/sigma-computing/reference/creategrant).
+
+## Teams Pattern
+
+Avoid sharing workspaces or workbooks directly with users. Always share with a team and assign users to the team. This keeps access management O(1) per user instead of O(workbooks × users).
+
+For automatic embed-user creation, the `teams` JWT claim auto-assigns the user to the listed teams on each session. If you change the claim values, Sigma overwrites the user's team membership on the next session.
+
+```js
+// Automatic embed user — claim drives team membership
+{ sub: 'user@example.com', teams: ['Customer A', 'Viewers'] }
+```
+
+```js
+// Internal user — team membership is managed inside Sigma; omit the claim
+{ sub: 'employee@company.example' /* no teams claim */ }
+```
+
+## Account Types
+
+Account types control feature-level permissions (export, schedule, save-as, etc.). Defaults: Viewer, Explorer, Creator, Admin. You can define custom account types.
+
+| Provisioning | How account type is set |
+|--------------|------------------------|
+| Automatic embed user | `account_type` JWT claim (omit and Sigma defaults to the highest available) |
+| Internal user | Set in Sigma admin or via IdP |
+
+## Granting Access — Programmatic API Path
+
+For new-customer onboarding workflows, the typical sequence (using API credentials from `sigma-api`):
+
+```sh
+# 1. Create a team for the new customer.
+curl -X POST "$SIGMA_BASE_URL/v2/teams" \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SIGMA_API_TOKEN" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"name": "Customer A"}'
+
+# 2. Grant the team access to the embed workbook.
+curl -X POST "$SIGMA_BASE_URL/v2/grants" \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SIGMA_API_TOKEN" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{
+    "grants": [{
+      "grantee": { "teamId": "<team-id>" },
+      "permission": "view",
+      "workbookId": "<workbook-id>"
+    }]
+  }'
+
+# 3. (Optional) Assign a user attribute value to the team for RLS / role switching.
+curl -X POST "$SIGMA_BASE_URL/v2/userAttributes/<attributeId>/teams" \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SIGMA_API_TOKEN" \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '[{ "teamId": "<team-id>", "value": "A" }]'
+```
+
+On each embed session, the JWT carries `teams: ["Customer A"]` and any required `user_attributes`; Sigma auto-creates the user, assigns them to the team, and resolves data access from the team's grants.
+
+## Internal Users — Critical Reminders
+
+When an internal user (someone with a Sigma login) accesses an embed:
+- Do **not** set `account_type`, `teams`, `first_name`, `last_name`, or `user_attributes` in their JWT.
+- Their account type, team membership, and attributes come from Sigma.
+- If you set those claims anyway, the internal user's Sigma-configured values may be overwritten or rejected.
+
+Common implementation: detect at JWT-signing time whether the `sub` is an internal email (e.g., from your domain) and emit only the required claims for that case.
